course: biology
lesson: 11
title: Swamps, marshes, mangroves and bogs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A wetland is land that is soaked with water for much of the year: a marsh is full of reeds and grasses, a swamp has trees standing in water, a bog is a cold wet sponge of moss, and a mangrove is a forest growing in salty mud by the sea.
explain: Squeeze a handful of bog moss and water pours out as if from a bath sponge; a whole bog works like that, holding rain and letting it go slowly.
explain: The hard thing about wetland mud is that it has almost no oxygen, so roots can suffocate and dead plants hardly rot; poke it with a stick and stinky bubbles of marsh gas come up.
explain: Wetland living things have clever answers: mangroves grow breathing roots like snorkels, sundews and flytraps catch insects for the nitrogen the soil lacks, and fish like mudskippers even walk out onto the mud.
explain: Wetlands are not wasteland: they soak up floods, clean water, and store huge amounts of carbon, so many countries now protect them and even build new ones.
fact: Some mangroves grow breathing roots called pneumatophores that stick straight up out of the mud and take in air through tiny pores, because the salty mud has almost no oxygen.
fact: Red mangrove seedlings start growing while still hanging on the parent tree, then drop like darts into the mud or float away; they can drift for months and still take root on a faraway shore.
fact: Sphagnum, or bog moss, has big empty cells that fill with water, so it can hold up to about 20 times its own dry weight, and it makes the bog wet and acidic.
fact: Peat is half-rotted dead plants, mostly bog moss, piled up over thousands of years; it grows only about 1 millimetre a year, because in cold, wet, acid, airless bogs rotting is very slow; peatlands cover only about 3 percent of the land but store more carbon than all the world's forests together.
fact: Bog soil has very little nitrogen, so the sundew catches insects on sticky drops and the Venus flytrap snaps shut on them, and both digest their prey to get nitrogen.
fact: A Venus flytrap closes only if its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds, so raindrops and dust do not waste its energy; in the wild it grows only in a small area of North and South Carolina in the USA.
fact: An alligator floats with only its eyes and nostrils above the water, because they sit on top of its head; the Florida Everglades is the only place where alligators and crocodiles live side by side.
fact: Herons, mudskippers and other animals with backbones each have just one heart.
fact: A grey heron stands perfectly still in shallow water, then shoots out its S-shaped neck like a spring to grab a fish or frog.
fact: Mudskippers are fish of mangrove swamps that skip across the mud on their fins; they keep water in their gill chambers and breathe through their wet skin, so they can stay out of water for hours.
fact: In airless marsh mud, bacteria rot dead plants and make methane and smelly hydrogen sulphide, the rotten-egg smell; old stories of will-o'-the-wisp lights may come from marsh gas, but scientists are not sure.
fact: A dragonfly lives one to several years as a nymph underwater, then climbs up a reed and its skin splits to let the winged adult out; the adult has 6 legs and 4 wings.
fact: Wetlands soak up flood water like giant sponges and let it out slowly, and reed roots trap mud and clean the water.
fact: Bogs and swamps can be dangerous to walk on, so always stay on paths and boardwalks, and never go near the water's edge where alligators live.
exercise: What are the mangrove roots that stick up out of the mud like snorkels for? | Taking in air, because the mud has almost no oxygen | Catching the little fish that swim between them | Drinking rainwater before it gets salty | Holding the tree up in strong winds only
exercise: What does a red mangrove seedling do before it falls? | It starts growing while still hanging on the parent tree | It dries out into a hard seed first | It waits inside a fruit for a whole year first | It grows a parachute to fly away
exercise: How much water can bog moss hold? | Up to about 20 times its own dry weight | About the same as its own weight | About twice its own weight, like a sponge | None, it is waterproof
exercise: What is peat made of? | Half-rotted dead plants, mostly bog moss | Black volcanic ash and sand | Old tree roots slowly turned into hard black stone | Mud dropped by rivers in big floods
exercise: How fast does peat grow? | About 1 millimetre a year | About 1 whole metre every year | About 1 centimetre every week | It does not grow at all, ever
exercise: Why does a sundew catch insects? | Bog soil has very little nitrogen, so it gets it from insects | It has no leaves to make food from light | It needs the insects' water to live through summer | It is protecting its flowers from being eaten
exercise: How many touches of its trigger hairs make a Venus flytrap snap shut? | Two within about 20 seconds | One is always enough | Ten touches in a row | It shuts every hour by itself, whether or not an insect is inside
exercise: Where do Venus flytraps grow in the wild? | Only in a small area of North and South Carolina | All over the hot, steamy rainforests of Brazil and Peru | In bogs all across Europe | In the deserts of Australia
exercise: How can an alligator see and breathe while almost hidden? | Its eyes and nostrils are on top of its head | It breathes through its skin while it is under the water | It holds its breath for whole days | It pokes a reed up to breathe through
exercise: What is special about the Florida Everglades? | Alligators and crocodiles live there side by side | It is the only place in the whole world where alligators live | It is a bog with no animals at all | It is the biggest desert in America
exercise: How does a grey heron catch a fish? | It stands still, then shoots out its S-shaped neck | It dives from high in the sky like a gannet | It chases fish underwater with its webbed feet | It scoops them up in its feet while flying low over the water
exercise: How can a mudskipper stay out of water for hours? | It keeps water in its gills and breathes through its wet skin | It has lungs just like a frog's | It holds one big breath before it leaves | It stops breathing until the tide comes back in
exercise: What makes the rotten-egg smell when you poke marsh mud? | Hydrogen sulphide made by bacteria rotting plants without oxygen | Eggs laid in the mud by marsh birds that have gone bad in the warm sun | Salt from the sea drying out | Sap leaking from crushed reeds
exercise: What comes out of a dragonfly nymph when it climbs up a reed? | The winged adult dragonfly, out of its split skin | A caterpillar that will spin a silky cocoon on the reed | A tadpole that will grow legs first and then grow wings | Hundreds of tiny dragonfly eggs that are ready to hatch
exercise: How many legs and wings does an adult dragonfly have? | 6 legs and 4 wings | 8 legs and 2 wings | 6 legs and 2 wings | 4 legs and 4 wings
exercise: How do wetlands help after a big storm? | They soak up the water and let it out slowly | They make the rain fall somewhere else instead | They push the flood water back out into the sea | They freeze the water so it cannot flow anywhere
exercise: Which store more carbon? | All the world's peat bogs | All the world's forests together | Both store exactly the same | Neither, bogs give off all their carbon
test: my cousin says swamps are just useless smelly land, better drained. right? | No, wetlands soak up floods, clean water and store lots of carbon | Yes, nothing useful lives in them | Yes, draining them always stops floods and gives farms | No, but only because they look pretty
test: A wooden tool and a lump of butter 2000 years old were dug out of an Irish bog in good shape. Why had they not rotted? | Bogs are cold, wet, acid and airless, so rotting is very slow | Someone had wrapped them in plastic | Peat is a kind of freezer kept below zero all year | The butter was made of something that never rots
test: In a cartoon, a Venus flytrap chases the hero through a jungle snapping at him. What is wrong? | It is a small plant of Carolina bogs that cannot move or eat people | Nothing, they hunt large animals in jungles | It should be chasing him underwater | It should be smaller, but it does live in jungles and can walk about slowly at night
test: What would happen to a Venus flytrap if every raindrop made it snap shut? | It would waste lots of energy closing on nothing | It would grow twice as fast | It would catch many more insects than before | Nothing, closing costs it no energy
test: Josh counts 3 herons and 2 mudskippers by the pond. How many hearts is that? | 5, each animal has one heart | 10, as each one has two hearts | 3, because fish have no hearts | 7, herons have two hearts each
test: crap there's like 4 dragonflies on the reeds, how many wings is that lol | 16 | 8 | 4 | 24
test: Mia wants to jump off the boardwalk to grab a sundew in the bog. What should she do? | Stay on the boardwalk, bogs can be deep and dangerous | Jump, bog moss is soft and always safe | Jump only if her boots are waterproof | Run across very quickly, so that she does not have time to sink
test: Why would a mangrove tree die if all its breathing roots were covered by thick mud? | Its buried roots would suffocate without air | It would get far too much fresh water | The salt would wash out of its leaves | Fish would eat its roots
test: Ben says a dragonfly is born with wings and flies right away. Is that right? | No, it lives underwater as a nymph, often for years, first | Yes, dragonflies never touch water | Yes, but only for one day | No, it starts as a caterpillar on a leaf and makes a cocoon in the reeds
test: Old stories tell of ghostly lights dancing over marshes at night. What do scientists say? | They may come from marsh gas catching fire, but it is not proven | They are definitely the ghosts of people who were lost in the bog long ago | They are proven to be fireflies in every case | They are reflections of the Moon on the mud
test: A mangrove seedling drops into the tide instead of the mud. What can happen? | It can float for months and take root on a faraway shore | It dies at once in the salty water | It sinks to the bottom and grows on the seabed among the fish and the coral | It turns back into a seed
test: why do bog plants bother eating flies, they got leaves innit | Their leaves make food from light, but the bog soil lacks nitrogen | They have no leaves and must eat to live | Flies give them water in dry summers | They only eat flies to stop the flies from stinging them and their flowers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain mangrove species develop erect aerial roots. | To obtain air, the saturated saline mud being almost devoid of oxygen | To capture marine organisms as a source of nourishment | To absorb fresh rainwater prior to its contamination by the salt of the tide | To repel herbivorous mammals from the base of the trunk
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of peat? | It accumulates at about one millimetre per annum from incompletely decomposed vegetation | It is deposited annually by rivers in flood at about one metre per annum | It is formed instantaneously by volcanic activity beneath bogs | It is composed principally of the remains of marine animals
exam: Ascertain the condition under which the trap of Dionaea muscipula, the Venus flytrap, is caused to close. | The stimulation of its trigger hairs twice within approximately twenty seconds | A single contact of any kind upon its outer surface, whether by an insect or by a drop of rain | The onset of darkness at the conclusion of each day | Any change in the temperature of the surrounding air
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of wetlands in respect of flooding. | They absorb flood water and subsequently release it gradually | They accelerate the passage of flood water towards settlements | They exert no influence whatsoever upon flooding | They cause precipitation to fall in neighbouring regions instead of upon the wetland
